From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] MLD STA: send Action frames on a link that has a channel

Depends on 889-MLD-STA-send-Action-frames-to-the-AP-MLD-on-an-enabl.patch
and on 892-nl80211-report-the-channel-of-an-MLD-link-in-channel.patch.

The SA Query Request and the WNM-Sleep Request stay on the association
link with an OCI. While a TID-to-link mapping disables that link, it has
no channel, so the OCI cannot be built and the frame is not sent. A
station with OCV then deauthenticates itself after an unprotected
Deauthentication frame. Build the OCI from the association link if it has
a channel, else from the first link that has one, and send the frame on
that link.

The FT Action frame carries no OCI, so it needs no fixed link. The WMM
ADDTS and DELTS and the TWT frames kept the frequency of the association
link, which the driver now refuses while that link is disabled. Send them
with no frequency, so that the driver picks an active link.

A Radio Measurement Report, a solicited DSCP Policy Response, a
Collocated Interference Report, a 20/40 BSS Coexistence frame and a WNM
Notification of a Beacon protection failure are not in the list of
frames intended for the MLD (IEEE Std 802.11be-2024, 35.3.14.1), so they
belong on one link:
- a report or a response goes on the receive link of its request,
- a 20/40 BSS Coexistence frame goes on the association link,
- the WNM Notification goes on the link whose BSSID the Beacon frame
  names as SA.
Take the channel of that link from the driver at the send, since a
stored frequency goes stale at a channel switch. If that link has no
channel, as under a mapping that disables it, do not send the frame.
Another link would need an MLO Link Info element, and an AP MLD discards
a frame whose element names a link that is not enabled (35.3.14.3). A
Collocated Interference Report with dialog token 0 answers no request and
goes with no frequency.

The receive link of a request was found by its frequency in
wpa_s->links[].freq. wpa_drv_get_mlo_info() set that value to 0 for a
link that has no channel when the station reads its links, and the value
goes stale on a link that hears no channel switch. Take the receive link
from the driver event (NL80211_ATTR_MLO_LINK_ID) instead. Where the
driver names no link, take the link whose current channel is the
frequency of the frame. The SA Query Response takes its frequency from
the current channel of that link.

wpa_drv_get_mlo_info() keeps the stored frequency of a link for which the
driver names no channel. The driver names no channel for a link until the
link is active, and mac80211_hwsim activates a link of the default
mapping only after the association event. With the stored value,
MLO_STATUS names the frequency of that link, not 0.

MGMT_RX_PROCESS of the control interface sets the link of its receive
event to -1, which driver.h defines for a frame with no receive link.
The event otherwise names link 0 for every frame.

The 20/40 BSS Coexistence frame reports on the BSS of the association
link: the OBSS scan, the secondary channel, the channels within 10 MHz of
assoc_freq and the Country element all come from current_bss. Another
link on 2.4 GHz belongs to another BSS.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
